WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.848
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:08.189 --> 00:00:09.880
MARC JORDAN: Ladies
and gentlemen,

00:00:09.880 --> 00:00:13.710
welcome to Google Cloud
Next and welcome to DEV206,

00:00:13.710 --> 00:00:17.610
Scaling with Cloud Identity
for Customers and Partners.

00:00:17.610 --> 00:00:19.860
My name is Marc Jordan,
I am a product manager

00:00:19.860 --> 00:00:23.550
in Google Cloud, and I literally
only think about identity.

00:00:23.550 --> 00:00:26.410
I'm joined today by my dear
friends and colleagues, Pierre

00:00:26.410 --> 00:00:29.580
and Francois, who I'll invite
to join me on the stage in just

00:00:29.580 --> 00:00:32.820
a moment, but I'm going to take
you through a little bit first.

00:00:32.820 --> 00:00:35.400
Francois, head of IT
operations, and Pierre,

00:00:35.400 --> 00:00:39.060
the chief technology
officer of Mailjet.

00:00:39.060 --> 00:00:40.770
So as I said, I'm
going to talk to you

00:00:40.770 --> 00:00:45.630
about the way we think of Google
Cloud's Identity Platform.

00:00:45.630 --> 00:00:48.450
I'm going to invite Pierre
to talk about Mailjet,

00:00:48.450 --> 00:00:50.490
and then Francois to
discuss how they've

00:00:50.490 --> 00:00:54.280
implemented Identity Platform
to scale their global business.

00:00:54.280 --> 00:00:56.280
Finally, we're round it
out with some questions.

00:00:56.280 --> 00:00:58.470
I encourage you all to ask
questions in the session

00:00:58.470 --> 00:01:00.210
or catch us afterwards
in the hallway.

00:01:00.210 --> 00:01:03.950
I'm happy to dive deeper,
happy to talk more.

00:01:03.950 --> 00:01:09.480
So Identity Platform-- going
back to December last year,

00:01:09.480 --> 00:01:11.970
we announced Cloud Identity
for Customers and Partners,

00:01:11.970 --> 00:01:16.320
or CICP as we endearingly
called it, as a public beta.

00:01:16.320 --> 00:01:18.700
And over the last
three months or so,

00:01:18.700 --> 00:01:21.180
we've seen phenomenal traction.

00:01:21.180 --> 00:01:22.830
We've had thousands
of developers,

00:01:22.830 --> 00:01:25.410
literally thousands of
developers come to our platform

00:01:25.410 --> 00:01:28.780
and onboard for all sorts
of different use cases.

00:01:28.780 --> 00:01:31.440
We've had people build
identity for things.

00:01:31.440 --> 00:01:34.570
So they've gone from
credentials on things

00:01:34.570 --> 00:01:37.690
out there in the wild to
short-lived tokens that

00:01:37.690 --> 00:01:40.420
are issued by our platform,
being able to interact

00:01:40.420 --> 00:01:43.810
with Google Cloud Platform to
sort of push up analytics, push

00:01:43.810 --> 00:01:47.190
up data, get things
from storage.

00:01:47.190 --> 00:01:50.420
We've had people build
MDM-style platforms,

00:01:50.420 --> 00:01:53.370
so logging onto Windows
and Macintosh machines,

00:01:53.370 --> 00:01:55.800
and using the platform
that we've built

00:01:55.800 --> 00:01:57.880
as the source for credentials.

00:01:57.880 --> 00:02:00.360
And we've had people build
next-gen intelligence platforms

00:02:00.360 --> 00:02:03.610
across Google Cloud Platform
and all of other Google--

00:02:03.610 --> 00:02:05.630
a set of Google services.

00:02:05.630 --> 00:02:07.350
And we took this feedback.

00:02:07.350 --> 00:02:09.600
We reveled in the fact that
these customers are really

00:02:09.600 --> 00:02:11.350
getting a lot of value
out of this service

00:02:11.350 --> 00:02:12.420
that we're providing.

00:02:12.420 --> 00:02:14.660
And we thought to ourselves,
this Cloud Identity

00:02:14.660 --> 00:02:17.250
for Customers and Partners,
it can surely do more than

00:02:17.250 --> 00:02:18.922
what we're positioning it as.

00:02:18.922 --> 00:02:20.630
And so we went back
to the drawing board,

00:02:20.630 --> 00:02:22.410
and I've got two big
announcements today.

00:02:22.410 --> 00:02:24.930
The first-- the service is
now generally available,

00:02:24.930 --> 00:02:27.000
and so I encourage
even more developers

00:02:27.000 --> 00:02:29.670
to start to bring their
production critical workloads

00:02:29.670 --> 00:02:33.070
to our service and have this
strong foundational platform.

00:02:33.070 --> 00:02:35.190
The second thing is we're
rebranding the platform

00:02:35.190 --> 00:02:36.520
as well.

00:02:36.520 --> 00:02:40.290
And so what was CICP
is now simply known

00:02:40.290 --> 00:02:41.675
as Identity Platform.

00:02:41.675 --> 00:02:43.050
And with that,
we're bringing all

00:02:43.050 --> 00:02:45.960
of the samples, all the
SDKs, all the documentation,

00:02:45.960 --> 00:02:48.300
all the things you'd
expect from a platform

00:02:48.300 --> 00:02:49.800
so you don't have
to build identity.

00:02:49.800 --> 00:02:51.810
You don't have to think
about cryptography.

00:02:51.810 --> 00:02:54.330
We have all of that
covered for you.

00:02:54.330 --> 00:02:57.860
So what is Identity Platform?

00:02:57.860 --> 00:03:01.520
And really, this is about taking
Google's extensive knowledge

00:03:01.520 --> 00:03:03.950
and giving it to
you, our developers,

00:03:03.950 --> 00:03:06.360
and to our customers
as a service.

00:03:06.360 --> 00:03:09.350
So you can take all the
lessons that Google's learned,

00:03:09.350 --> 00:03:11.600
all the battle hardening,
all the stress testing

00:03:11.600 --> 00:03:14.390
that we've done over decades
hosting consumer services,

00:03:14.390 --> 00:03:17.750
and build that
into your services.

00:03:17.750 --> 00:03:20.363
So we break it down into
a few different pillars.

00:03:20.363 --> 00:03:22.030
First and foremost,
we want to offer you

00:03:22.030 --> 00:03:24.290
Google-grade authentication.

00:03:24.290 --> 00:03:28.480
So it's based on widely
adopted Google infrastructure.

00:03:28.480 --> 00:03:31.900
We have eight services with
over a billion users each,

00:03:31.900 --> 00:03:34.540
and you don't get there by
learning a few things about how

00:03:34.540 --> 00:03:36.370
to manage identities securely.

00:03:36.370 --> 00:03:38.680
And so, as I said, delivering
that to our customers

00:03:38.680 --> 00:03:40.820
is our number one priority.

00:03:40.820 --> 00:03:44.350
Second, we want to give you
customizable open source

00:03:44.350 --> 00:03:47.350
and open standards so you
can build on top of it

00:03:47.350 --> 00:03:50.020
and customize it with
your own look and feel.

00:03:50.020 --> 00:03:52.723
If you want your logo displayed
all the way through the login

00:03:52.723 --> 00:03:54.640
experience, all the way
into your application,

00:03:54.640 --> 00:03:56.560
you should absolutely do it.

00:03:56.560 --> 00:03:58.840
If you want the color
red from start to finish,

00:03:58.840 --> 00:04:00.460
you should absolutely
do that as well.

00:04:00.460 --> 00:04:03.190
So you'll never see a Google
logo, Google log-in screens,

00:04:03.190 --> 00:04:05.320
or anything like that
when you use this service.

00:04:05.320 --> 00:04:07.450
But we do give you things
like sign-in widgets

00:04:07.450 --> 00:04:10.450
to enable you to use some
of Google's best practices

00:04:10.450 --> 00:04:12.970
to help people onboard,
to stop dropoff,

00:04:12.970 --> 00:04:16.870
to help adoption of your
product and your service.

00:04:16.870 --> 00:04:19.149
We have a broad support for
authentication protocols,

00:04:19.149 --> 00:04:23.060
so OpenID Connect, SAML,
OAuth, those sort of things

00:04:23.060 --> 00:04:25.390
we support out of the box.

00:04:25.390 --> 00:04:28.690
And we give you client SDKs
across iOS, Android, and web,

00:04:28.690 --> 00:04:30.190
so you have to build
authentication.

00:04:30.190 --> 00:04:32.648
You don't have to think about
how to interact with our APIs

00:04:32.648 --> 00:04:35.710
in order to get tokens to sign
users in and things like that.

00:04:35.710 --> 00:04:39.010
And that's great for serverless,
modern-style applications.

00:04:39.010 --> 00:04:40.870
But we understand
that, in many cases,

00:04:40.870 --> 00:04:43.000
you're probably moving a
service to Google Cloud

00:04:43.000 --> 00:04:46.700
Platform or some other platform,
and you need back-end SDKs.

00:04:46.700 --> 00:04:47.910
You've got existing servers.

00:04:47.910 --> 00:04:49.780
You want to be able
to validate tokens.

00:04:49.780 --> 00:04:51.640
You want to be able to do
things in a secure and trusted

00:04:51.640 --> 00:04:52.280
environment.

00:04:52.280 --> 00:04:54.790
So we give you server SDKs
across all the languages

00:04:54.790 --> 00:04:55.540
you might expect--

00:04:55.540 --> 00:04:59.930
Python, Go, Java,
the list goes on.

00:04:59.930 --> 00:05:01.430
Now, it wouldn't
be a Google product

00:05:01.430 --> 00:05:04.220
if we didn't deliver you
advanced user security.

00:05:04.220 --> 00:05:07.160
And so with Google, we deliver--

00:05:07.160 --> 00:05:09.050
cross-account protection
is one of the ways

00:05:09.050 --> 00:05:11.135
that we help to protect
Google accounts.

00:05:11.135 --> 00:05:13.010
And we deliver that if
you build your service

00:05:13.010 --> 00:05:15.000
on top of Identity Platform.

00:05:15.000 --> 00:05:18.055
So what that means is that,
if a Google account, be

00:05:18.055 --> 00:05:20.180
an Enterprise account, a
consumer account, whatever

00:05:20.180 --> 00:05:22.670
it may be, is signed
into any service sitting

00:05:22.670 --> 00:05:24.798
on top of Identity
Platform, the service

00:05:24.798 --> 00:05:26.840
gets a notification that
this account is actively

00:05:26.840 --> 00:05:27.440
under attack.

00:05:27.440 --> 00:05:29.000
Someone's threatening
this account,

00:05:29.000 --> 00:05:31.940
and can log the user out,
force them to reauthenticate,

00:05:31.940 --> 00:05:35.010
audit the activity in your
logs, so you can take action.

00:05:35.010 --> 00:05:36.590
So you can use
Google's intelligence

00:05:36.590 --> 00:05:38.882
to help protect all of the
applications you're building

00:05:38.882 --> 00:05:40.895
on top of this service.

00:05:40.895 --> 00:05:42.770
We're soon to offer
two-factor authentication

00:05:42.770 --> 00:05:44.160
to make that even stronger.

00:05:44.160 --> 00:05:45.830
And so we really want to
roll that out and make sure

00:05:45.830 --> 00:05:47.883
that we, first and foremost,
deliver things that

00:05:47.883 --> 00:05:50.300
are important and meet customers
where they are, but start

00:05:50.300 --> 00:05:51.528
to do revolutionary things.

00:05:51.528 --> 00:05:53.570
And so you can see some
of the interesting things

00:05:53.570 --> 00:05:55.520
we're doing there in
some of the sessions

00:05:55.520 --> 00:05:58.822
tomorrow around security keys
and other bits and pieces.

00:05:58.822 --> 00:06:01.280
And finally, we're built on
Google's secure infrastructure.

00:06:01.280 --> 00:06:03.920
You've probably heard it a
whole lot in the last few hours,

00:06:03.920 --> 00:06:05.030
at least, and you'll
hear it a lot more

00:06:05.030 --> 00:06:06.270
over the next couple of days.

00:06:06.270 --> 00:06:08.810
But everything from
the chips in the bottom

00:06:08.810 --> 00:06:11.390
of our servers all the
way up to the data centers

00:06:11.390 --> 00:06:14.180
and to the APIs that are going
to be exposed to your end users

00:06:14.180 --> 00:06:18.330
is using Google's
secure infrastructure.

00:06:18.330 --> 00:06:22.020
And we want to deliver
this at planet scale.

00:06:22.020 --> 00:06:23.445
We are a global service.

00:06:23.445 --> 00:06:25.320
And we hear over and
over from our customers,

00:06:25.320 --> 00:06:27.150
like, hey, I don't
want to have to spin up

00:06:27.150 --> 00:06:29.820
a database in Asia
East, and I don't have

00:06:29.820 --> 00:06:31.722
to spin up a database in EMEA.

00:06:31.722 --> 00:06:33.430
I want this delivered
where my users are.

00:06:33.430 --> 00:06:36.570
One user might be in one place
at one point and somewhere else

00:06:36.570 --> 00:06:38.160
at another point in time.

00:06:38.160 --> 00:06:40.830
Don't make me have to figure
out architecture and things

00:06:40.830 --> 00:06:41.610
like that.

00:06:41.610 --> 00:06:44.250
Offer it through a
global service with CDNs

00:06:44.250 --> 00:06:46.930
where the user is with databases
that are local to the user.

00:06:49.650 --> 00:06:51.140
No one just buys Identity.

00:06:51.140 --> 00:06:52.378
Identity's not sexy.

00:06:52.378 --> 00:06:54.920
I want it to integrate with all
the things I'm already using.

00:06:54.920 --> 00:06:56.430
So make it work with functions.

00:06:56.430 --> 00:06:57.930
Log it into Stackdriver.

00:06:57.930 --> 00:06:59.610
Start to use other
services that I'm

00:06:59.610 --> 00:07:01.770
familiar with that I'm
already using, and help

00:07:01.770 --> 00:07:05.370
paint this end-to-end solution
throughout the entire journey

00:07:05.370 --> 00:07:07.500
my user's taking.

00:07:07.500 --> 00:07:11.130
And, most importantly,
you're building a service

00:07:11.130 --> 00:07:13.890
so that your users can
interact with your business.

00:07:13.890 --> 00:07:16.840
If they can't log in, they can't
interact with your business.

00:07:16.840 --> 00:07:18.882
So it doesn't matter how
great your features are,

00:07:18.882 --> 00:07:20.882
if they can't get through
the front door, that's

00:07:20.882 --> 00:07:21.750
a real problem.

00:07:21.750 --> 00:07:22.733
And we understand that.

00:07:22.733 --> 00:07:24.150
So we're offering
industry leading

00:07:24.150 --> 00:07:28.892
SLA's of 99.95% and
Enterprise-grade support.

00:07:28.892 --> 00:07:30.600
So if you're using
Google Cloud Platform,

00:07:30.600 --> 00:07:32.808
you have one number to call,
and they can support you

00:07:32.808 --> 00:07:36.230
with all of the things that
are important in our service.

00:07:36.230 --> 00:07:39.230
So it's great for me to wave my
hands around and kind of talk

00:07:39.230 --> 00:07:39.920
about it.

00:07:39.920 --> 00:07:41.380
Let's show you through it.

00:07:41.380 --> 00:07:44.700
So if we want to
flip to the demo.

00:07:44.700 --> 00:07:45.950
This is Google Cloud Platform.

00:07:45.950 --> 00:07:49.320
If you're not familiar
with it, it's pretty great.

00:07:49.320 --> 00:07:50.660
And in here.

00:07:50.660 --> 00:07:52.200
I'm going to go to
the marketplace.

00:07:52.200 --> 00:07:53.658
And so as I said,
Identity Platform

00:07:53.658 --> 00:07:55.200
is generally available today.

00:07:55.200 --> 00:07:57.050
And so if I want
to onboard to it,

00:07:57.050 --> 00:07:59.360
I can quite simply go
to the Marketplace,

00:07:59.360 --> 00:08:02.870
go to Identity Platform,
and simply enable it.

00:08:02.870 --> 00:08:03.810
That's it.

00:08:03.810 --> 00:08:06.050
So once the service
is onboarded,

00:08:06.050 --> 00:08:07.890
once you're enabled
for this service,

00:08:07.890 --> 00:08:10.170
there's all sorts of
things you can do with it.

00:08:10.170 --> 00:08:11.877
Here's one I prepared earlier.

00:08:11.877 --> 00:08:14.210
So we support a range of
different providers, as I said.

00:08:14.210 --> 00:08:17.900
So we support open standards,
OpenID Connect, and SAML,

00:08:17.900 --> 00:08:19.910
a range of different
social providers,

00:08:19.910 --> 00:08:21.830
as well as signing
in with what users

00:08:21.830 --> 00:08:23.570
have, emails and
passwords and things,

00:08:23.570 --> 00:08:25.070
as well as anonymous,
which I'm just

00:08:25.070 --> 00:08:27.140
going to pause on for a second.

00:08:27.140 --> 00:08:32.120
Don't ever store information
about a user you don't need to.

00:08:32.120 --> 00:08:34.100
You want to help build
a trust in your brand

00:08:34.100 --> 00:08:36.809
and help have a user have trust
in what they're getting access

00:08:36.809 --> 00:08:37.309
to.

00:08:37.309 --> 00:08:38.090
So start light.

00:08:38.090 --> 00:08:39.757
As soon as they access
your application,

00:08:39.757 --> 00:08:42.530
you might want to sort start
to remember who they are,

00:08:42.530 --> 00:08:44.810
start to keep tabs on
what they're doing.

00:08:44.810 --> 00:08:47.450
And then over time, as they
maybe they get to a checkout,

00:08:47.450 --> 00:08:49.070
maybe they want to put
their credit card number in

00:08:49.070 --> 00:08:50.028
or something like that.

00:08:50.028 --> 00:08:51.737
At that point, then
you can step them up.

00:08:51.737 --> 00:08:53.450
So you can upgrade
that anonymous account

00:08:53.450 --> 00:08:55.783
that you previously created
that's been collecting state

00:08:55.783 --> 00:08:58.610
on that user, and then turn them
into a paid user, or a named

00:08:58.610 --> 00:09:00.510
user, or something like
that in the future.

00:09:00.510 --> 00:09:01.927
So this is really,
really critical

00:09:01.927 --> 00:09:04.760
to building that trust, to
build that progressive profiling

00:09:04.760 --> 00:09:06.380
sort of chain.

00:09:06.380 --> 00:09:09.710
And so once users sign
in the first time,

00:09:09.710 --> 00:09:12.440
we then obviously have a
representation of the account.

00:09:12.440 --> 00:09:14.300
Now, you'll hear from
the Mailjet team, what

00:09:14.300 --> 00:09:17.120
was really important
to us was people

00:09:17.120 --> 00:09:18.735
have accounts systems today.

00:09:18.735 --> 00:09:20.360
We want to interact
with what you have.

00:09:20.360 --> 00:09:21.830
We want to integrate
with the services

00:09:21.830 --> 00:09:22.830
that you've already got.

00:09:22.830 --> 00:09:26.270
So you can migrate users
with their hashed passwords

00:09:26.270 --> 00:09:27.680
directly into our
service, or you

00:09:27.680 --> 00:09:30.680
can create brand new accounts
with SAML and OpenID Connect.

00:09:30.680 --> 00:09:31.970
And there's no need to create.

00:09:31.970 --> 00:09:35.400
These will happen just
in time if you so desire.

00:09:35.400 --> 00:09:37.910
So let's quickly flip to a demo.

00:09:37.910 --> 00:09:41.360
So as I said, this is a
completely white-labeled

00:09:41.360 --> 00:09:44.010
service, so I can do
whatever I want with it.

00:09:44.010 --> 00:09:46.790
So I might want to choose
to sign in with Google,

00:09:46.790 --> 00:09:48.800
sign in with Facebook,
brand this page the way

00:09:48.800 --> 00:09:50.090
that I see fit.

00:09:50.090 --> 00:09:52.790
I might also want to brand
it completely differently,

00:09:52.790 --> 00:09:54.860
support things like
SAML, OpenID Connect,

00:09:54.860 --> 00:09:58.270
maybe integrate with my existing
Active Directory environment.

00:09:58.270 --> 00:10:03.690
And so I'm going to
sign in here and use

00:10:03.690 --> 00:10:07.450
my super secure password.

00:10:07.450 --> 00:10:10.200
And we're going to give
you a JSON web token back.

00:10:10.200 --> 00:10:12.140
And so, in this JSON
web token, we're

00:10:12.140 --> 00:10:15.360
then going to give you custom
claims, information about what

00:10:15.360 --> 00:10:18.960
we're storing for that user
so that then you can take that

00:10:18.960 --> 00:10:20.520
into a system downstream.

00:10:20.520 --> 00:10:23.460
You might want to get
access to something running

00:10:23.460 --> 00:10:26.340
inside of Google Cloud,
maybe a different cloud.

00:10:26.340 --> 00:10:28.613
Anything that's got our
SDKs in it works just fine.

00:10:28.613 --> 00:10:30.030
The one thing
that's critical here

00:10:30.030 --> 00:10:31.713
is that we also
directly integrate

00:10:31.713 --> 00:10:33.880
with the Anthos announcement
you heard this morning.

00:10:33.880 --> 00:10:35.713
So, if you're looking
to modernize your SaaS

00:10:35.713 --> 00:10:39.150
applications, it's really about
starting with a strong Identity

00:10:39.150 --> 00:10:39.690
foundation.

00:10:39.690 --> 00:10:41.190
And so while you're
modernizing, you

00:10:41.190 --> 00:10:44.970
can look to move in place to
a modern Identity solution

00:10:44.970 --> 00:10:47.150
and kind of go from there.

00:10:47.150 --> 00:10:48.600
So that's the end of the demo.

00:10:55.640 --> 00:10:58.990
So, as I said, we're
generally available today.

00:10:58.990 --> 00:11:00.560
And without further
ado, I'd love

00:11:00.560 --> 00:11:03.320
to welcome my two co-presenters
to the stage, Francois

00:11:03.320 --> 00:11:06.050
and Pierre, who'll discuss a
little bit more about Mailjet

00:11:06.050 --> 00:11:08.540
and its use of
Identity Platform.

00:11:08.540 --> 00:11:10.100
PIERRE PUCHOIS: Hello, everyone.

00:11:10.100 --> 00:11:12.540
So, first of all, thank
you very much to be here.

00:11:12.540 --> 00:11:16.310
It's a pleasure to share
that experience with you.

00:11:16.310 --> 00:11:18.580
So, first, we'll talk
about the implementation,

00:11:18.580 --> 00:11:22.370
and we'll introduce the
project on the Mailjet side.

00:11:22.370 --> 00:11:26.530
I'm sorry for my French accent.

00:11:26.530 --> 00:11:30.600
So the agenda for the
presentation-- so first of all,

00:11:30.600 --> 00:11:35.000
I'm going to talk about the
whole backbone or architecture.

00:11:35.000 --> 00:11:40.040
Then I will talk about why
we talked about the user

00:11:40.040 --> 00:11:42.110
management, why it's
important for us,

00:11:42.110 --> 00:11:45.440
and especially why we
choose Google for that.

00:11:45.440 --> 00:11:48.110
And Francois will talk
about the implementation

00:11:48.110 --> 00:11:52.340
and especially the feedbacks
we can give to you today.

00:11:52.340 --> 00:11:55.010
And mainly, the bigger
objective is really

00:11:55.010 --> 00:11:57.710
to share our
experience with you,

00:11:57.710 --> 00:12:02.560
so there is a Q&amp;A
session just after.

00:12:02.560 --> 00:12:04.270
So who we are--

00:12:04.270 --> 00:12:07.630
we are emailing email
service provider.

00:12:07.630 --> 00:12:12.250
We send all over the world more
than 2 billion email comments.

00:12:12.250 --> 00:12:15.010
We sent marketing and
production emails.

00:12:15.010 --> 00:12:17.410
And there is a huge
probability that you

00:12:17.410 --> 00:12:21.610
have an email we sent to
you recently or actually is

00:12:21.610 --> 00:12:24.550
doing the response.

00:12:24.550 --> 00:12:29.920
The platform is really key for
us regarding the sending speed

00:12:29.920 --> 00:12:31.670
rate, which is something
really important,

00:12:31.670 --> 00:12:35.680
especially on the
[INAUDIBLE] side.

00:12:35.680 --> 00:12:41.170
We also have a big
marketing ecosystem

00:12:41.170 --> 00:12:43.000
on top of the platform.

00:12:43.000 --> 00:12:48.970
And we have a
complete set of API.

00:12:48.970 --> 00:12:52.630
Let's say that all
our architecture

00:12:52.630 --> 00:12:54.100
is reachable for the API.

00:12:58.170 --> 00:13:01.530
So this is the global picture
of our key architecture.

00:13:01.530 --> 00:13:03.240
As you can see, we
have first of all,

00:13:03.240 --> 00:13:07.350
[INAUDIBLE] layer so data
center network server SMTP.

00:13:07.350 --> 00:13:10.110
On top of that, we have
all the API ecosystem

00:13:10.110 --> 00:13:12.130
private and public.

00:13:12.130 --> 00:13:14.920
As I said, all our let's
say, core application

00:13:14.920 --> 00:13:16.980
reachable for the API.

00:13:16.980 --> 00:13:19.930
And we have three
ecosystem on top of these.

00:13:19.930 --> 00:13:22.950
The first one is the, let's
say, the marketing ecosystem

00:13:22.950 --> 00:13:28.730
with a lot of different features
and applications, [INAUDIBLE]..

00:13:28.730 --> 00:13:32.250
All you need to send your
beautiful newsletters.

00:13:32.250 --> 00:13:34.480
And so this is the
first ecosystem.

00:13:34.480 --> 00:13:36.940
The second one is
the API ecosystem.

00:13:36.940 --> 00:13:39.540
So you can reach directly
all our functionalities

00:13:39.540 --> 00:13:41.760
directly through the API.

00:13:41.760 --> 00:13:45.010
And the third one is the, let's
say, integration ecosystem,

00:13:45.010 --> 00:13:47.580
which is really important
for us because we

00:13:47.580 --> 00:13:51.750
have a more and
more big partners,

00:13:51.750 --> 00:13:54.720
then we have to integrate.

00:13:54.720 --> 00:13:58.560
So you can imagine that using
that kind of architecture,

00:13:58.560 --> 00:14:02.130
we need a very huge
and strong and studied

00:14:02.130 --> 00:14:03.180
user management system.

00:14:08.770 --> 00:14:10.410
So what is a CIAM?

00:14:10.410 --> 00:14:12.380
Customer Identity and
Access Management.

00:14:12.380 --> 00:14:14.960
So you probably already
know about that.

00:14:14.960 --> 00:14:17.910
Marc explained that just before.

00:14:17.910 --> 00:14:22.420
But as a quick remember, it's
a service to manage users.

00:14:22.420 --> 00:14:24.460
Let's say it's user database.

00:14:24.460 --> 00:14:26.430
It's also a solution
to authenticate user

00:14:26.430 --> 00:14:29.020
on the platform on the solution.

00:14:29.020 --> 00:14:32.260
And it's a set of feature to
power the user experience.

00:14:32.260 --> 00:14:34.710
So Marc talked about that.

00:14:34.710 --> 00:14:37.080
We have a two-step
authentication for example.

00:14:37.080 --> 00:14:38.950
A deep log, SSO, et
cetera, et cetera.

00:14:42.670 --> 00:14:48.170
So user management is
everything, especially for us.

00:14:48.170 --> 00:14:48.670
Why?

00:14:48.670 --> 00:14:53.890
Because we provide services
in B to B and B to B to C. So

00:14:53.890 --> 00:14:59.450
we have to face multiple
user integrations issues.

00:14:59.450 --> 00:14:59.950
Why?

00:14:59.950 --> 00:15:02.710
Because each time we have
to integrate a partner,

00:15:02.710 --> 00:15:05.710
we have to take care about the
authentication and the identity

00:15:05.710 --> 00:15:06.980
management.

00:15:06.980 --> 00:15:09.790
And, at this point,
all the project

00:15:09.790 --> 00:15:11.000
are completely different.

00:15:11.000 --> 00:15:14.440
So we have to develop a
dedicated platform, a dedicated

00:15:14.440 --> 00:15:17.396
solution to fit the needs.

00:15:17.396 --> 00:15:21.240
So going to [INAUDIBLE] why
it's really important for us

00:15:21.240 --> 00:15:23.190
is regarding the security.

00:15:23.190 --> 00:15:28.060
The security is really strategic
regarding the user management.

00:15:28.060 --> 00:15:32.850
I think it's quite logical, but
we have to take care of this.

00:15:32.850 --> 00:15:37.440
And also managing as we
do now, local IAM solution

00:15:37.440 --> 00:15:41.140
is really complex and
requires multiple skills,

00:15:41.140 --> 00:15:44.680
especially on the dev side.

00:15:44.680 --> 00:15:50.590
And now probably you face
exactly the same needs than us.

00:15:50.590 --> 00:15:53.920
We have more and more customer
asking for a social log ins.

00:15:53.920 --> 00:15:58.850
So something also we want
to provide to our customers.

00:15:58.850 --> 00:16:01.030
And it's quite
complicated when we have

00:16:01.030 --> 00:16:04.320
to develop that by ourselves.

00:16:04.320 --> 00:16:06.870
And last point,
why it's important?

00:16:06.870 --> 00:16:09.390
Because the sign up
process has to really

00:16:09.390 --> 00:16:11.700
be fast and easy,
otherwise, you could

00:16:11.700 --> 00:16:15.200
lose in terms of efficiency.

00:16:15.200 --> 00:16:16.720
Some figures we
are being measured.

00:16:16.720 --> 00:16:20.405
So more than the 400K users.

00:16:20.405 --> 00:16:24.000
And we have a, as you can see,
more than 8K daily sign ins.

00:16:24.000 --> 00:16:26.700
So user management is
really critical for us.

00:16:29.820 --> 00:16:33.420
So why we decided
to move on the tour,

00:16:33.420 --> 00:16:36.340
let's say, revamp the solution.

00:16:36.340 --> 00:16:39.950
First, because though as I said,
it's really strategic for us

00:16:39.950 --> 00:16:42.810
because we are the SAS platform.

00:16:42.810 --> 00:16:45.830
So we are really directly
concerned by this kind

00:16:45.830 --> 00:16:47.990
of solution.

00:16:47.990 --> 00:16:50.380
As I said also, we have
more and more partners.

00:16:50.380 --> 00:16:54.230
And the having a new
partner on our platform

00:16:54.230 --> 00:16:58.940
it's really costly because we
have to develop a dedicated

00:16:58.940 --> 00:16:59.630
solution.

00:16:59.630 --> 00:17:02.510
So it's not quite easy.

00:17:02.510 --> 00:17:04.540
And regarding the
time to market,

00:17:04.540 --> 00:17:06.240
it's absolutely not good.

00:17:06.240 --> 00:17:10.770
So that's why we really
wanted to review complete 3D

00:17:10.770 --> 00:17:13.530
solution this architecture.

00:17:13.530 --> 00:17:15.500
We also have more
and more customers

00:17:15.500 --> 00:17:19.579
asking for an authentication
directly on their directory.

00:17:19.579 --> 00:17:22.069
For example, we have
a big luxury brand,

00:17:22.069 --> 00:17:25.579
I cannot say the name here,
but it's a French one.

00:17:25.579 --> 00:17:28.610
And it starts with a
C. You will understand.

00:17:28.610 --> 00:17:32.120
So they really want to
connect directly their users

00:17:32.120 --> 00:17:36.500
to our platform without
managing user-owned passwords.

00:17:36.500 --> 00:17:40.252
I can understand in terms of
security is good for them.

00:17:40.252 --> 00:17:42.710
So that's why we also have to
provide this kind of service.

00:17:45.230 --> 00:17:50.000
The last point, but I'm pretty
sure we are all agree on this,

00:17:50.000 --> 00:17:52.730
our set of features
is really limited.

00:17:52.730 --> 00:17:55.820
It's limited because we have
to develop that internally.

00:17:55.820 --> 00:17:57.830
And usually it's
not the first topic

00:17:57.830 --> 00:18:01.430
we want to address when we
developed a new project.

00:18:01.430 --> 00:18:04.470
So in terms of a
two-factor authentication,

00:18:04.470 --> 00:18:07.010
we really want to
provide distribution.

00:18:07.010 --> 00:18:10.020
It becomes a stand out
now in the web industry.

00:18:10.020 --> 00:18:13.040
We also want to
provide social log ins.

00:18:13.040 --> 00:18:19.400
And we will also need activity
logs on the audit capabilities

00:18:19.400 --> 00:18:22.990
because we are ISO
27K certified under.

00:18:22.990 --> 00:18:26.730
And regarding the audit
we face if we are.

00:18:26.730 --> 00:18:29.750
It's something really important.

00:18:29.750 --> 00:18:33.020
And we are not
directly concerned now,

00:18:33.020 --> 00:18:34.400
but we are thinking about that.

00:18:34.400 --> 00:18:36.410
The mobile
reconstitution is also

00:18:36.410 --> 00:18:41.780
something we want to
provide to our customers.

00:18:41.780 --> 00:18:44.710
So why did we choose Google?

00:18:44.710 --> 00:18:45.380
Good question.

00:18:45.380 --> 00:18:46.870
It took time.

00:18:46.870 --> 00:18:49.450
First of all, just
to decide that we

00:18:49.450 --> 00:18:52.360
will able to externalize
that solution,

00:18:52.360 --> 00:18:54.490
because it's really
sensitive, it's critical.

00:18:54.490 --> 00:18:58.690
So sometimes it's quite
hard to externalize.

00:18:58.690 --> 00:19:02.620
So first, we decided to
go for an externalization.

00:19:02.620 --> 00:19:06.050
And then we had to
choose the partner.

00:19:06.050 --> 00:19:07.210
So why Google?

00:19:07.210 --> 00:19:09.020
For many reasons.

00:19:09.020 --> 00:19:12.590
So first, because we
are listed on the GCP.

00:19:12.590 --> 00:19:15.100
So a [INAUDIBLE].

00:19:15.100 --> 00:19:17.470
We go natively.

00:19:17.470 --> 00:19:20.260
We looked at the
Google solutions.

00:19:20.260 --> 00:19:23.860
Also because there
is a Google provide

00:19:23.860 --> 00:19:29.320
a lot of functionalities as
Marc presented just before.

00:19:29.320 --> 00:19:31.480
Also, because it's
really easy to integrate.

00:19:31.480 --> 00:19:36.470
And you will see it took us less
than six months to integrate

00:19:36.470 --> 00:19:39.170
IP for the identity platform--

00:19:39.170 --> 00:19:39.670
Sorry.

00:19:39.670 --> 00:19:44.200
That's the ICP-- in
the now solution.

00:19:44.200 --> 00:19:47.320
And it's so it's scalable,
which is also something

00:19:47.320 --> 00:19:49.180
really important for us.

00:19:49.180 --> 00:19:50.570
We are growing fast.

00:19:50.570 --> 00:19:55.440
So we really need to have a
natively scalable solution.

00:19:55.440 --> 00:19:59.670
And also, for the
security, I think

00:19:59.670 --> 00:20:02.760
Google is really strong
in terms of security.

00:20:02.760 --> 00:20:05.010
[INAUDIBLE] for sure.

00:20:05.010 --> 00:20:09.180
So that's why we decided
to go to with Google.

00:20:09.180 --> 00:20:14.370
Two cons-- let's talk
about that just one minute.

00:20:14.370 --> 00:20:16.470
The vendor dependence--
so you have

00:20:16.470 --> 00:20:18.960
to accept that you are
linked with Google.

00:20:18.960 --> 00:20:22.500
So it's something you
have to deal with.

00:20:22.500 --> 00:20:27.120
And you also have to
accept to externalize

00:20:27.120 --> 00:20:29.550
that kind of
solution, and to have

00:20:29.550 --> 00:20:31.530
a core service in your
architecture based

00:20:31.530 --> 00:20:34.650
on the external PasS solution.

00:20:34.650 --> 00:20:36.550
So it depends on your business.

00:20:36.550 --> 00:20:41.420
You have to think about that,
having the pros and the cons.

00:20:41.420 --> 00:20:44.580
But for our case,
we definitively

00:20:44.580 --> 00:20:46.690
decided to go with Google.

00:20:46.690 --> 00:20:50.250
And we did the implementation
in a very easy way, I would say.

00:20:53.940 --> 00:20:55.770
We can talk about
that a bit later on

00:20:55.770 --> 00:20:59.145
if you want to go more into the
details regarding the decision.

00:21:02.711 --> 00:21:05.076
Francois.

00:21:05.076 --> 00:21:08.550
FRANCOIS FANUEL:
Thank you, Pierre.

00:21:08.550 --> 00:21:10.650
So regarding the
implementation--

00:21:14.100 --> 00:21:18.360
so as Pierre said, we have
a lot of different use cases

00:21:18.360 --> 00:21:26.340
to integrate customers with
a similar user database.

00:21:26.340 --> 00:21:29.270
Some want an id connect.

00:21:29.270 --> 00:21:32.260
Other want to use
a G Suite account,

00:21:32.260 --> 00:21:36.300
and we still have
our own user database

00:21:36.300 --> 00:21:41.770
that we manage today with
logins and passwords.

00:21:41.770 --> 00:21:45.380
So when you have to do
everything, it's complicated.

00:21:45.380 --> 00:21:46.800
It takes time.

00:21:46.800 --> 00:21:48.300
Hard to secure.

00:21:48.300 --> 00:21:50.700
It's not really flexible.

00:21:50.700 --> 00:21:57.690
And if we dig into details,
this is oh, it works today.

00:21:57.690 --> 00:22:02.580
The yellow parts are what
we manage there directly.

00:22:02.580 --> 00:22:06.615
So yeah, it's a huge
ecosystem to manage.

00:22:09.270 --> 00:22:13.440
It's also hard to yeah--

00:22:13.440 --> 00:22:16.200
there is not a lot
of value into that.

00:22:16.200 --> 00:22:23.850
So it's really hard for
us to dedicate developers

00:22:23.850 --> 00:22:25.680
developing identity solutions.

00:22:25.680 --> 00:22:34.950
So, in this architecture,
all the connections

00:22:34.950 --> 00:22:41.460
with external directories
or with our partners

00:22:41.460 --> 00:22:45.080
is something we
wanted to externalize.

00:22:45.080 --> 00:22:49.960
So, if we go to the
target architecture--

00:22:49.960 --> 00:22:51.820
in the target
architecture the system

00:22:51.820 --> 00:22:56.150
is really much more simpler.

00:22:56.150 --> 00:23:07.550
We just have to integrate
with identity platform SDKs

00:23:07.550 --> 00:23:13.130
all user database
at Google and create

00:23:13.130 --> 00:23:18.620
connector between our partners
and the Googles authentication

00:23:18.620 --> 00:23:19.550
system.

00:23:19.550 --> 00:23:22.310
So we still have to
distribute tokens

00:23:22.310 --> 00:23:25.280
for our partner integrations.

00:23:25.280 --> 00:23:29.280
We still have to also
have some lookups

00:23:29.280 --> 00:23:31.980
if we want to integrate
with enterprise directories.

00:23:31.980 --> 00:23:34.490
I will talk about that later.

00:23:34.490 --> 00:23:37.640
But still, the system are
really much, much simpler.

00:23:37.640 --> 00:23:43.190
You have just one SDK on your
web application to integrate.

00:23:43.190 --> 00:23:49.640
And on our side, we
also use the REST API

00:23:49.640 --> 00:23:57.840
to directly integrate identity
platform admin features.

00:24:00.750 --> 00:24:04.370
So on the client side,
we use the web SDK, which

00:24:04.370 --> 00:24:08.510
is super easy to integrate.

00:24:08.510 --> 00:24:10.110
And we use the REST API.

00:24:10.110 --> 00:24:14.210
So you will see into
the documentation

00:24:14.210 --> 00:24:18.870
it talks a lot about the SDKs,
but not a lot about the REST

00:24:18.870 --> 00:24:19.370
API.

00:24:19.370 --> 00:24:22.180
So there is a REST API.

00:24:22.180 --> 00:24:24.750
The features are complete.

00:24:24.750 --> 00:24:26.540
So you can use it.

00:24:26.540 --> 00:24:28.490
And that's what we choose to do.

00:24:28.490 --> 00:24:30.440
On the server side,
for [INAUDIBLE]

00:24:30.440 --> 00:24:33.440
you will use the REST API also.

00:24:33.440 --> 00:24:36.470
In this project
we choose to first

00:24:36.470 --> 00:24:41.030
just do a drop in replacement of
our main authentication system.

00:24:41.030 --> 00:24:44.730
So only the user
password system.

00:24:44.730 --> 00:24:48.180
And add the other
features after.

00:24:48.180 --> 00:24:51.810
This way, we were
able to develop

00:24:51.810 --> 00:24:55.120
really fast or this project.

00:24:55.120 --> 00:24:58.320
And adding other
features is really simple

00:24:58.320 --> 00:25:01.170
because it's already prepared
the identity platform.

00:25:03.930 --> 00:25:06.210
In terms of timeline--

00:25:06.210 --> 00:25:11.490
so, along this year, like
two months this year,

00:25:11.490 --> 00:25:16.060
because of what Pierre said,
really a lot of pros and cons.

00:25:16.060 --> 00:25:18.870
So we had to decide wisely.

00:25:18.870 --> 00:25:22.560
And then the implementation
was really fast.

00:25:22.560 --> 00:25:26.400
Architectural design
in January, development

00:25:26.400 --> 00:25:27.960
in February and March.

00:25:27.960 --> 00:25:31.260
And as now, the product
is generally available.

00:25:31.260 --> 00:25:33.720
We'll be able to launch.

00:25:33.720 --> 00:25:37.800
We decided to not launch
within the beta period

00:25:37.800 --> 00:25:42.430
because this is all
when the policy is,

00:25:42.430 --> 00:25:45.340
but now it's
generally available.

00:25:50.250 --> 00:25:53.190
Some implementation
tips-- so when

00:25:53.190 --> 00:25:55.860
you do a project
like that, you have

00:25:55.860 --> 00:25:59.550
to keep in mind that you have
your own database on our side.

00:25:59.550 --> 00:26:06.570
It's something like 400K users
to migrate from one system

00:26:06.570 --> 00:26:08.310
to another.

00:26:08.310 --> 00:26:14.880
And unfortunately, when we
choose several years ago,

00:26:14.880 --> 00:26:20.880
we choose the proprietary
encryption [INAUDIBLE] system

00:26:20.880 --> 00:26:26.550
for passwords, which
is not supported

00:26:26.550 --> 00:26:31.230
by Google because there are a
lot of [INAUDIBLE] algorithm

00:26:31.230 --> 00:26:33.330
available.

00:26:33.330 --> 00:26:35.050
But [INAUDIBLE] the other ones.

00:26:35.050 --> 00:26:39.420
So we had to find
a way to make what

00:26:39.420 --> 00:26:43.640
our users without any
service interruption.

00:26:43.640 --> 00:26:47.970
And so resetting
passwords for everybody

00:26:47.970 --> 00:26:50.650
was not something we can do.

00:26:50.650 --> 00:27:01.090
So what we decided to do is
to do a two-step migration.

00:27:01.090 --> 00:27:03.190
First step, during
your long period,

00:27:03.190 --> 00:27:08.960
we will collect user passwords
when they log into our system.

00:27:08.960 --> 00:27:11.700
So the workflow is like that.

00:27:11.700 --> 00:27:14.150
A Mailjet user
authenticates around

00:27:14.150 --> 00:27:16.660
our current internal database.

00:27:16.660 --> 00:27:20.515
And if the password is
varied, we will generate.

00:27:20.515 --> 00:27:24.100
We will sign up the user
to identity platform

00:27:24.100 --> 00:27:27.400
with this password.

00:27:27.400 --> 00:27:31.980
And when we will have something
like 80% of all database,

00:27:31.980 --> 00:27:35.400
migrating like that we'll
be able to switch and say

00:27:35.400 --> 00:27:41.190
to the 20% that
didn't [INAUDIBLE]

00:27:41.190 --> 00:27:43.650
during the migration
phase, that they'll

00:27:43.650 --> 00:27:46.290
have to change their password.

00:27:46.290 --> 00:27:50.130
So there are a lot
of ways to do that.

00:27:50.130 --> 00:27:54.330
We decided to do that
like that, because we

00:27:54.330 --> 00:28:05.060
didn't want it to lose a lot of
users' time when they log in.

00:28:05.060 --> 00:28:08.010
In identity platform,
there are quotas.

00:28:08.010 --> 00:28:11.850
So the quotas are
well documented.

00:28:11.850 --> 00:28:15.360
But you cannot always ask for
a temporary increase if you

00:28:15.360 --> 00:28:17.820
generate a lot of users--

00:28:17.820 --> 00:28:19.740
of sign ups.

00:28:19.740 --> 00:28:22.350
There is in the console
something to do that.

00:28:26.380 --> 00:28:32.760
Another thing's that great
in identity platform is logs.

00:28:32.760 --> 00:28:37.070
So you have activity logs
for basically everything.

00:28:37.070 --> 00:28:42.980
And they are mostly
free for the most part.

00:28:42.980 --> 00:28:45.210
Use it a lot.

00:28:45.210 --> 00:28:48.380
It's really helpful
to ease the debug.

00:28:48.380 --> 00:28:52.850
It's also really helpful
for auditability.

00:28:52.850 --> 00:28:57.470
So, as Pierre said, we
are ISO 27K satisfied.

00:28:57.470 --> 00:29:05.210
We have to keep a lot of logs
due to this certification.

00:29:05.210 --> 00:29:07.380
So it's really helpful.

00:29:07.380 --> 00:29:08.615
And it's accessible through--

00:29:11.570 --> 00:29:14.990
If you use Google Cloud,
it's really easy to access

00:29:14.990 --> 00:29:17.990
like, old user logs.

00:29:17.990 --> 00:29:23.330
For REST API, the [INAUDIBLE].

00:29:23.330 --> 00:29:26.750
So if I ever comment
on this product,

00:29:26.750 --> 00:29:30.230
it was really great to
integrate, great to really fast

00:29:30.230 --> 00:29:31.820
to integrate.

00:29:31.820 --> 00:29:36.860
Docs are sometimes
a bit hard to find.

00:29:36.860 --> 00:29:39.320
So with the revamp
of the name, it

00:29:39.320 --> 00:29:42.280
may be interesting to
consolidate everything.

00:29:44.930 --> 00:29:48.570
There is a REST API
and the docs are there.

00:29:51.580 --> 00:29:57.850
Finally, just to show you we
revamped the authentication

00:29:57.850 --> 00:29:59.030
page.

00:29:59.030 --> 00:30:05.250
So as you can see, we
have social log ins

00:30:05.250 --> 00:30:07.270
on the top of the page.

00:30:07.270 --> 00:30:10.120
And we'll probably
change a bit this page

00:30:10.120 --> 00:30:13.360
when we will integrate
the federation

00:30:13.360 --> 00:30:20.500
with other directories
by removing the password,

00:30:20.500 --> 00:30:22.540
the password box.

00:30:22.540 --> 00:30:26.520
What we want to do is
provide a seamless experience

00:30:26.520 --> 00:30:27.650
around users.

00:30:27.650 --> 00:30:32.310
So we'll probably just ask
them to put their email,

00:30:32.310 --> 00:30:35.200
and check in their
own database which

00:30:35.200 --> 00:30:40.240
federated identity provider
is used by this user

00:30:40.240 --> 00:30:44.710
before redirecting him
to the right system.

00:30:44.710 --> 00:30:49.150
This way we'll be able
to easily redirect

00:30:49.150 --> 00:30:52.040
the user is using
federated identities

00:30:52.040 --> 00:30:54.730
or ask them for a password
in a similar phase.

00:30:54.730 --> 00:31:00.500
So we probably have a two-step
authentication phase to provide

00:31:00.500 --> 00:31:01.990
a seamless experience.

00:31:01.990 --> 00:31:08.650
But you can also just put a
button like, in the [INAUDIBLE]

00:31:08.650 --> 00:31:13.160
to ask for a federated
identity provider.

00:31:15.780 --> 00:31:16.280
Marc.

00:31:20.000 --> 00:31:23.570
MARC JORDAN: Thank you so much.

00:31:23.570 --> 00:31:27.140
So we just did general
availability today.

00:31:27.140 --> 00:31:29.302
And this is really
just the start for us.

00:31:29.302 --> 00:31:30.760
Thanks so much for
the Mailjet team

00:31:30.760 --> 00:31:32.860
for sort of getting on there
and giving us feedback.

00:31:32.860 --> 00:31:34.810
And so we've heard that loud
and clear some of the things

00:31:34.810 --> 00:31:35.560
that they've said.

00:31:35.560 --> 00:31:37.000
And so we continue to innovate.

00:31:37.000 --> 00:31:39.250
And I'm pretty excited to
show them some of the things

00:31:39.250 --> 00:31:41.167
we've got coming out
in the near future.

00:31:41.167 --> 00:31:43.000
But even beyond that,
we've got so much more

00:31:43.000 --> 00:31:45.080
that we want to do.

00:31:45.080 --> 00:31:47.810
And so we offer strong
authentication today.

00:31:47.810 --> 00:31:49.870
How can we make
authentication so strong

00:31:49.870 --> 00:31:53.320
that users are never impacted by
the authentication experience,

00:31:53.320 --> 00:31:55.600
but they have the strongest
possible authentication

00:31:55.600 --> 00:31:56.390
mechanisms?

00:31:56.390 --> 00:31:59.170
So we're really passionate
about protecting end users,

00:31:59.170 --> 00:32:01.870
making sure that they have
this beautiful experience end

00:32:01.870 --> 00:32:03.160
to end into your application.

00:32:03.160 --> 00:32:04.690
They're only prompted
for authentication

00:32:04.690 --> 00:32:05.440
when they need to.

00:32:05.440 --> 00:32:07.180
And when they do
go through that,

00:32:07.180 --> 00:32:11.390
it's through the strongest
mechanisms possible.

00:32:11.390 --> 00:32:14.000
More extensibility--
we are a platform.

00:32:14.000 --> 00:32:17.187
So how can we deliver
even more extensibility?

00:32:17.187 --> 00:32:19.770
Google can be opinionated about
some of the things that we do.

00:32:19.770 --> 00:32:22.672
But how can we offer the
right solutions for you?

00:32:22.672 --> 00:32:25.130
And you say, well, I really
like this-- sort of these three

00:32:25.130 --> 00:32:27.680
building blocks together, but
I need to put these extra two

00:32:27.680 --> 00:32:28.743
pieces on top of it.

00:32:28.743 --> 00:32:31.160
And we're doing all sorts of
interesting stuff integrating

00:32:31.160 --> 00:32:33.243
across Google Cloud with
some of the announcements

00:32:33.243 --> 00:32:35.900
you'll hear this week, and some
future announcements to make

00:32:35.900 --> 00:32:37.358
sure that you have
the right pieces

00:32:37.358 --> 00:32:40.100
to extend sort of
wherever you want to go,

00:32:40.100 --> 00:32:42.830
whether it be hybrid
environments, on prem,

00:32:42.830 --> 00:32:45.175
more GCP, other services,
and things like that.

00:32:45.175 --> 00:32:46.550
So we're investing
really heavily

00:32:46.550 --> 00:32:49.278
in our extensibility story.

00:32:49.278 --> 00:32:50.570
And finally, more integrations.

00:32:50.570 --> 00:32:53.450
As I said, there's great
integrations with things

00:32:53.450 --> 00:32:55.910
like Function, Stackdriver.

00:32:55.910 --> 00:32:57.590
We're doing a lot of
work at the moment

00:32:57.590 --> 00:33:00.590
with other parts of the business
that are really interesting.

00:33:00.590 --> 00:33:03.960
And so once you have a user,
knowing the user's identity

00:33:03.960 --> 00:33:05.330
is extremely valuable.

00:33:05.330 --> 00:33:07.760
And then being able to
sort of track that journey

00:33:07.760 --> 00:33:09.710
be able to feed that
into dependent systems,

00:33:09.710 --> 00:33:12.080
be able to take action,
be able to understand,

00:33:12.080 --> 00:33:14.120
perform analytics,
and things like that.

00:33:14.120 --> 00:33:16.140
These integrations
are really important,

00:33:16.140 --> 00:33:18.530
not from a security
perspective necessarily,

00:33:18.530 --> 00:33:20.450
although they are
valuable, but it's really

00:33:20.450 --> 00:33:22.220
about getting the most
value you possibly

00:33:22.220 --> 00:33:24.710
can out of having users
access your system,

00:33:24.710 --> 00:33:25.970
understanding their behaviors.

00:33:25.970 --> 00:33:30.190
And so we think that's
really important.

00:33:30.190 --> 00:33:31.690
So it is a price to product.

00:33:31.690 --> 00:33:34.250
And pricing kicks in at
general availability.

00:33:34.250 --> 00:33:37.240
We do offer a pretty
generous free tier

00:33:37.240 --> 00:33:40.360
if you do want to get started
and get playing with it.

00:33:40.360 --> 00:33:41.870
And these are list prices.

00:33:41.870 --> 00:33:44.132
So if you do have
an account rep,

00:33:44.132 --> 00:33:45.590
please tell them
to reach out to me

00:33:45.590 --> 00:33:48.650
and happy to have
a conversation.

00:33:48.650 --> 00:33:51.220
So as I demonstrated
in the demo,

00:33:51.220 --> 00:33:52.660
I encourage you to go try it.

00:33:52.660 --> 00:33:54.430
The free tier is
really, really generous.

00:33:54.430 --> 00:33:55.940
I'm going to give it a test.

00:33:55.940 --> 00:33:59.830
If you are staying on board
to GKE, GKE On Prem, Istio,

00:33:59.830 --> 00:34:03.010
all of those bits and pieces,
this is a fantastic service

00:34:03.010 --> 00:34:04.413
to help you board
to that quickly

00:34:04.413 --> 00:34:05.830
if you want to
Google stack that's

00:34:05.830 --> 00:34:10.300
going to work, or start
to take these identities

00:34:10.300 --> 00:34:12.500
and use them wherever
you have go to service.

00:34:12.500 --> 00:34:15.550
